# Routh II.Art.141 — the invariable line is absolutely fixed in space, and the
# instantaneous axis is not — dynamic notebook
#
# E. J. Routh, "The Advanced Part of a Treatise on the Dynamics of a System of
# Rigid Bodies", Part II (Macmillan, 6th ed. 1905), Art. 141: the plane of the
# impulsive couple that would set the body moving "is the Invariable plane and
# the normal to it the Invariable line. This line is absolutely fixed in space".
#
# THE TWO LINES. A torque-free body has two natural axes and they are easy to
# confuse:
#
#   the INVARIABLE LINE     -- the direction of the angular momentum. Fixed in
#                              space, because with no couple dL/dt = 0. It is
#                              NOT fixed in the body: it wanders through the
#                              body as the body turns, tracing the invariable
#                              cone of Art. 159.
#   the INSTANTANEOUS AXIS  -- the direction of the angular velocity. Fixed in
#                              NEITHER frame in general. It traces the polhode
#                              in the body and the herpolhode in space.
#
# Art. 141 also records the direction cosines of the invariable line in the
# body as A*w1/G, B*w2/G, C*w3/G -- explicitly functions of the w's, hence
# explicitly moving in the body, even though the line itself has not stirred.
#
# WHAT THE CELLS SHOW. A cuboid is set tumbling and two unit vectors are
# printed side by side, repeatedly:
#
#     normalize(angular_momentum)   -- the invariable line, in space
#     normalize(angular_velocity)   -- the instantaneous axis, in space
#
# The first does not move at all. The second moves a great deal. It is worth
# seeing them printed together, because "the axis of rotation" is the phrase
# most likely to be applied loosely to whichever of them the reader had in mind.
#
# THE NUMBERS. A cuboid of mass 3 with half-extents (0.5, 1, 2) gives
# I = diag(5, 4.25, 1.25). Started with w = (0.3, 3, 0.1):
#
#     L = I w = (1.5, 12.75, 0.125)      |L| = 12.838540610209558
#     invariable line = normalize(L) = (0.11683..., 0.99310..., 0.00973...)
#
# and that unit vector is reproduced to the last digit at every sample below,
# while normalize(w) swings visibly.
#
# The disturbance is asymmetric so the motion sits clearly off the separatrix
# G^2 = B*T of Art. 184 (here G^2 - B*T = +0.3) and the body genuinely tumbles.
#
# SETUP NOTE. system.g_constant = 0. Only one body is present, so gravity would
# do nothing here -- but in any multi-body version of this test, leaving G = 1
# lets the bodies fall together into the 1/r^2 singularity and ruins a purely
# rotational result for an entirely translational reason.
#
# WHAT YOU WATCH: a box tumbling in a complicated way about an axis that never
# stops moving, while the direction that actually matters never moves at all.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LAUNCH (run from the repository root: /Users/nsh/Developer/code/rust/rustSimulate)
#
#     posim_notebook routh_p2_invariable_line
#
# or, without the shell alias, the always-available form:
#
#     cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p2_invariable_line.posim
#
# The scene window opens in your browser. To start the simulation, press Start
# in that window, or type this at the In[] prompt in the terminal:
#
#     scene start
#
# If no browser should be launched (the URL is printed instead), use:
#
#     POSIM_NO_BROWSER=1 cargo run -p posim --release -- --notebook dynamic_notebooks/routh_p2_invariable_line.posim
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

set system.g_constant = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15

new cuboid { mass = 3, half_extents = [0.5, 1, 2], angular_velocity = [0.3, 3, 0.1] }

get obj0.angular_momentum
normalize(obj0.angular_momentum)
normalize(obj0.angular_velocity)

run 2 steps 2
normalize(obj0.angular_momentum)
normalize(obj0.angular_velocity)

run 2 steps 2
normalize(obj0.angular_momentum)
normalize(obj0.angular_velocity)

run 3 steps 3
normalize(obj0.angular_momentum)
normalize(obj0.angular_velocity)
reset

set system.g_constant = 0
set system.rtol = 1e-13
set system.atol = 1e-15
new cuboid { mass = 3, half_extents = [0.5, 1, 2], angular_velocity = [0.3, 3, 0.1] }

scene create
scene set_time_step 0.03
